  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its acceleration is $\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Which differential equation describes this motion?",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14097535/assets/f03ad90557b4bf978ece899892cada200873e920c8e00a0c196728108fee55c4.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we obtain the differential equation\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$",
